cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata below."
type: software
title: "A formally verified hexadecimal digit extraction for pi with an explicit guard for every position (Lean 4 development)"
authors:
  - family-names: Taylor
    given-names: Brian Bernard
version: "1.0.1"
# date-released: set to the actual v1.0.1 tag date at release time
date-released: "2026-11-01"
# Code: Apache-2.0 (LICENSE). The paper text in paper/ is CC-BY-4.0.
license: Apache-2.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/metasecdev/math-proofs"
# After Zenodo mints the DOI, add the concept DOI here:
# doi: "10.5281/zenodo.XXXXXXX"
keywords:
  - pi
  - BBP formula
  - digit extraction
  - irrationality measure
  - Lean 4
  - Mathlib
  - formal verification
abstract: >-
  Lean 4 / Mathlib development, checked with only the axioms propext, Classical.choice
  and Quot.sound (no sorry, no native_decide). It proves that pi equals the
  Bailey-Borwein-Plouffe series; that a guarded natural-number certificate [lo, hi],
  whenever lo = hi, returns floor(16^n * pi) and hence the n-th hexadecimal digit of pi;
  an explicit irrationality-measure bound for pi at the denominators 16^k
  (|16^k pi - p| * 16^(570k) > 2^-7182802 * 16^k for all integers p and k >= 1); and, as
  a consequence, that every guard depth g >= 569n + floor(log16(n+1)) + 1 + 1795710,
  in particular every g >= 570n + 1795711, returns the correct digit at position n, for
  every n. It also contains kernel-checked results on runs, distances and the guard depth
  needed at individual positions, and an analysis of a capped certificate: correct for
  n <= 2000 (checked in CI) and n <= 10000 (separate heavy library, checked by a local
  build), and provably wrong on an explicit window of positions near 2^272; positions
  between these ranges are unchecked for the capped certificate.
